#{[17592186378573 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-j+3k)-(3i+k) \\\\  & =-3i-j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i-2j+2k)-(3i+k) \\\\  & =-2i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+2j+k)-(3i+k) \\\\  & =-i+2j \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3((-2)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1)+((-2)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 1)+2((-2)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =6+1-12 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-5|=5.\n$$"] [17592186379355 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{k}^{2} & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot (-1))+2(1\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 0)+5(1\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}(6+4)+2(2)+5(-1) \\\\  & =10{k}^{2}+4-5 \\\\  & =10{k}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =0 \\\\ 10{k}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 10{k}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if \n$k=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}$ or $k=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{10}}.$"] [17592186382256 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-18\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot (2+k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=\\frac{1}{18}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{18}(2+k) & =\\frac{1}{18} \\\\ 2+k & =-1 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186382847 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y) & y{z}^{2} & (x-z)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y{z}^{2})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))]k \\\\  & =(0-2yz)i-(1-0)j+(0-\\frac{1}{x+y})k \\\\  & =-2yz\\,i-j-\\frac{1}{x+y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(2,-1,3),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(2,-1,3) & =-2(-1)(3)\\,i-j-\\frac{1}{2+(-1)}\\,k \\\\  & =6\\,i-j-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186383216 :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -8 & 10 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{3}.\n$$"] [17592186383258 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ x & 2y & 3z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)]k \\\\  & =(0-0)\\,i-(0-0)\\,j+(0-0)\\,k \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186383265 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {x}^{2} & xz & {z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-x)i-(0-0)j+(z-0)k \\\\  & =-x\\,i+z\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(-2,-3,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-2,-3,1) & =-(-2)\\,i+(1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186383272 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 2xy+2z & {y}^{2}+1 & x+y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}+1)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xy+2z)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+1)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+2z)]k \\\\  & =(1-0)i-(1-2)j+(0-2x)k \\\\  & =\\,i+j-2x\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(-3,0,-1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-3,0,-1) & =i+\\,j-2(-3)\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+6\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186383314 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & ={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)(y-1) \\\\ z-0 & =2(y-1) \\\\ z & =2y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=2y-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-2$ (or $2y-z-2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j+2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,2⟩.$"] [17592186383568 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{u-v} & -\\frac{1}{u-v} \\\\ -2u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u-v}\\cdot 1-(-2u)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{u-v}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2u}{u-v} \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(2,1)} & ={\\left(\\frac{1-2u}{u-v}\\right)\\,|}_{u=2\\,v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2(2)}{2-1} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-3|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =3\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186383575 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2v \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot 2v \\\\  & =(1-2v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-3,3)} & =(1-2v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\,{|}_{u=-3\\,v=3} \\\\  & =(1-2(3))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3+3) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-5|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =5\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186385302 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=-9\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\ 0 & -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =27{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)-(-27{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =27{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+27{cos}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =27[{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)] \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 0 & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-0 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{-9\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{27}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186385442 :question/worked-solution "Since $det(A)=det(B)$, the row operation applied to $A$ must be to add or subtract a multiple of one row to another row. In other words, it is of the form \n\n$$\n{R}_{j}:={R}_{j}+(k){R}_{i},\\,i\\ne j.\n$$\n\nBy considering the given row operations that are of the above form, we notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =det(B).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required row operation is\n\n$$\n{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3}.\n$$"] [17592186385494 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & ={f}_{y}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)(y-0) \\\\ z-2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ z &=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-\\frac{1}{2}y+2$ (or $y+2z-4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-2,1⟩.$"] [17592186385913 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3},$ and the first partial derivatives exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field on ${R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 0 & xy{e}^{z} & yz{e}^{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{z})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz{e}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(0)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{z})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)]k \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}-xy{e}^{z})i-(yz{e}^{x}-0)j+(y{e}^{z}-0)k \\\\  & =(z{e}^{x}-xy{e}^{z})\\,i-yz{e}^{x}\\,j+y{e}^{z}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{curl}\\,F$ at the point $(0,2,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(0,2,1) & =(1\\cdot {e}^{0}-0\\cdot 2\\cdot {e}^{1})\\,i-2\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{0}\\,j+2\\cdot {e}^{1}\\,k \\\\  & =i-2j+2e\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186385920 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4{e}^{2x} & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4 & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[(8{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+8{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))-2(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+4(2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[8-2(0)+4(2)] \\\\  & =16{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{16{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\int [{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386175 :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ x & 0 & yz\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[1(yz)-0(0)]i-[-2x(yz)-0(x)]j+[-2x(0)-1(x)]k \\\\  & =yz\\,i+2xyz\\,j-x\\,k \\\\  & =⟨yz,2xyz,-x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(y-{x}^{2})⟨z,0,0⟩+⟨yz,2xyz,-x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨z(y-{x}^{2}),0,0⟩+⟨yz,2xyz,-x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨z(2y-{x}^{2}),2xyz,-x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(0,2,-1) & =⟨(-1)(2(2)-(0)^{2}),2(0)(2)(-1),-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386459 :question/worked-solution "We first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these vectors when $u=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{3},\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,-2\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parameters $u=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ correspond to the point \n\n$$\n(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right),\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))=(1,\\sqrt{3},0),\n$$\n\nthe equation of the tangent plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-2,\\,-2\\sqrt{3},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-\\sqrt{3},\\,z⟩ & =0 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot (x-1)-2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (y-\\sqrt{3})+0\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ -2x-2\\sqrt{3\\,}y+8 & =0 \\\\ -x-\\sqrt{3}\\,y+4 & =0 \\\\ x+\\sqrt{3}\\,y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386466 :question/worked-solution "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\,{u}^{2},\\,{v}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2u,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨2,\\,2(1),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point where $u=1$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,\\frac{1}{2})\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-2,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386473 :question/worked-solution "In vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨1+{e}^{u},\\,2-{e}^{v},\\,1-{e}^{u}-{e}^{v}⟩.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+{e}^{u}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2-{e}^{v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-{e}^{u}-{e}^{v})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{u},\\,0,\\,-{e}^{u}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+{e}^{u}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2-{e}^{v}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-{e}^{u}-{e}^{v})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-{e}^{v},\\,-{e}^{v}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,-1) & =⟨{e}^{1},\\,0,\\,-{e}^{1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨e,\\,0,\\,-e⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,-1) & =⟨0,\\,-{e}^{-1},\\,-{e}^{-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point where $u=1$ and $v=-1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,-1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,-1) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ e & 0 & -e \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-1} & -{e}^{-1}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -e \\\\ -{e}^{-1} & -{e}^{-1}\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}e & -e \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-1}\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-1}\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386480 :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,-v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡v & -v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & -v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\ 0 & u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v+uv\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386487 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $(2,1,1)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1+v &=2 \\\\ uv &=1 \\\\ {u}^{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,v,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1+v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(uv),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,u,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,1) & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,1) & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point $(2,1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,1) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186386501 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $(1,1,2)$ lies on the surface, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ {v}^{2} &=1 \\\\ {u}^{2}-v &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(u,v) & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2v,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate these tangent vectors when $u=1$ and $v=-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}(1,-1) & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′}(1,-1) & =⟨0,\\,2(-1),\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-2,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={r}_{u}^{′}(1,-1)\\times {r}_{v}^{′}(1,-1) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,1,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387810 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{t},0,\\sqrt{3-t}⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-\\sqrt{3-t},0,-\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -\\sqrt{t} & 0 & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & 0 & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{t} & \\sqrt{3-t} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & -\\sqrt{t}\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{t} & 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3-t} & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0-0,-(t+(3-t)),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨0,-3,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387817 :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{4t}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,0,\\,\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(-2t)^{2}+{(0)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{2}{{(1+4{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩}{\\left(\\frac{2}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨1,0,2t⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}⟨-2t,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2t \\\\ -2t & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0-0,-(1+4{t}^{2}),0-0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}⟨0,\\,-(1+4{t}^{2}),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387971 :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\times g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} & {t}^{3} & t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & t\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{2} & t\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & {t}^{3}\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =⟨-4{t}^{6},\\,4{t}^{5}-t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩, \\\\ f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ t & 5 & {t}^{4} \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & {t}^{4} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}t & {t}^{4} \\\\ 2t & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}t & 5 \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =⟨5-3{t}^{6},\\,2{t}^{5}-t,\\,3{t}^{3}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\times g)^{′}(t) & =⟨-4{t}^{6},\\,4{t}^{5}-t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩+⟨5-3{t}^{6},\\,2{t}^{5}-t,\\,3{t}^{3}-10t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7{t}^{6}+5,\\,6{t}^{5}-2t,\\,4{t}^{3}-10t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388006 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3z-11=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x-2y-5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,-2,0⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\times ⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6i+6j-4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388013 :question/worked-solution "First, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $5x-2z+13=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨5,0,-2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z-25=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,-2⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4i+15j+10k \\\\  & =⟨4,15,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388020 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $2x=6$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=1-y$ (or $y+z=1$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388176 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}8 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388177 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388178 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 8 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3 & 3 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388179 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 19 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388180 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 19 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388190 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & \\sqrt{7} & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 11 & 0 & -9 & 11\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388191 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 4 & \\sqrt{5} & 3 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 3 & 0 & 11\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388192 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 4 & \\sqrt{5} & 3 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 3 & 0 & 11\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388193 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 4 & \\sqrt{5} & 8 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 3 & 0 & 11\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388194 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & \\sqrt{7} & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 11 & 0 & -9 & 11\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388216 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (2F+G) & =∇\\times (2F)+∇\\times G \\\\  & =2∇\\times F+∇\\times G\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ xyz & xy & {z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz)]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-xy)j+(y-xz)k \\\\  & =xy\\,j+(y-xz)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (2F+G) & =2∇\\times F+∇\\times G \\\\  & =2[xy\\,j+(y-xz)\\,k]+xy\\,j-x\\,k \\\\  & =2xy\\,j+2(y-xz)\\,k+xy\\,j-x\\,k \\\\  & =3xy\\,j+(2y-2xz-x)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(-1,2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n(∇\\times (2F+G))(-1,2,0)=-6\\,j+5\\,k.\n$$"] [17592186388223 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following linearity properties for curl:\n\n- $∇\\times (kF)=k∇\\times F$\n\n- $∇\\times (F+G)=∇\\times F+∇\\times G$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F+G) & =∇\\times F+∇\\times G\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\times F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {x}^{2}z & xz & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}z)]k \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x)i-(0-{x}^{2})j+(z-0)k \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x)\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j+z\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (F+G) & =∇\\times F+∇\\times G \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x)\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j+z\\,k+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{z}^{2}\\,j-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,k \\\\  & =-x\\,i+({x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,j+(z-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating at the point $(0,1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\n(∇\\times (F+G))(0,1,2)=-4\\,j+2\\,k.\n$$"] [17592186388230 :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & {z}^{2} & 2yz \\\\ 0 & x & 2\\sqrt{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[({z}^{2})(2\\sqrt{y})-(x)(2yz)]i-[(0)(2\\sqrt{y})-(0)(2yz)]j+[(0)(x)-(0)({z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(2{z}^{2}\\sqrt{y}-2xyz)\\,i+0\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2{z}^{2}\\sqrt{y}-2xyz,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =(y{z}^{2})⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}},0,1⟩+⟨2{z}^{2}\\sqrt{y}-2xyz,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2}\\sqrt{y},0,y{z}^{2}⟩+⟨2{z}^{2}\\sqrt{y}-2xyz,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{z}^{2}\\sqrt{y}-2xyz,0,y{z}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)(0,4,1)=⟨6,0,4⟩.\n$$"] [17592186388251 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -11 & 7 & 9 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186388251 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{32}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -5 & -4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -11 & 7 & 9 & -6\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-2){C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 & -6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 7 & -5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 & -6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 7 & -5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388253 :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 & 15 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388254 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -5 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388255 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -6 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -4 & -5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388256 :answer/value "-2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 9 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -10 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388257 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 7 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186388632 :question/worked-solution "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+8y &=-14 \\\\ 5x+4y &=13.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$ we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -14 \\\\ 5 & 13\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, according to Cramer's rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}=\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -14 \\\\ 5 & 13\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186388634 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -14 \\\\ 5 & 13\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186388635 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -14 \\\\ 5 & 13\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186388636 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 17\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186388637 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}-14 & 2 \\\\ 13 & 5\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186388638 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}-14 & 2 \\\\ 13 & 5\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186389126 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={y}^{3}+1\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(y)=3{y}^{2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+(3{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186389216 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 1-3{u}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{u+v} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}(1-3{u}^{2}) \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,2)} & ={e}^{u+v}(1-3{u}^{2})\\,{|}_{u=-2\\,v=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2+2}(1-3(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-11|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =11\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390360 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 5)i-(1\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)j+(1\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =(0-15)i-(3-6)j+(5-0)k \\\\  & =⟨-15,3,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390737 :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(f\\times g)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6t,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t)\\times g(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 12t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3{t}^{2} & (4t-1)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & (4t-1)\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}12t & 0 \\\\ 3 & (4t-1)\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}12t & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3{t}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =⟨8t-2,\\,12t-48{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}-6⟩ \\\\ f(t)\\times {g}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 6{t}^{2} & 2t & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6t & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 6 \\\\ 6t & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}6{t}^{2} & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}6{t}^{2} & 2t \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =⟨-28t,\\,-24{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\times g)^{′}(t) & =⟨8t-2,\\,12t-48{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}-6⟩+⟨-28t,\\,-24{t}^{2},\\,36{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-20t-2,\\,-72{t}^{2}+12t,\\,72{t}^{3}-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390793 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 11 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186390794 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 11 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186390795 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 11 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186390796 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 11 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186390797 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 11 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186390798 :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}\\times f)^{′}(t)={f}^{″}(t)\\times f(t)+{f}^{′}(t)\\times {f}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t\\,,\\,-3{t}^{2}\\,,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ {f}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\,,\\,-6t\\,,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(t)\\times f(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -6t & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-{t}^{3} & 1-t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6t & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{3} & 1-t\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-t\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -6t \\\\ 1-{t}^{2} & 1-{t}^{3}\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2-2t\\,,\\,-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ {f}^{′}(t)\\times {f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2} & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3{t}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -3{t}^{2} & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2t & -1 \\\\ -2t & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2t & -3{t}^{2} \\\\ -2t & -3{t}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =⟨0\\,,\\,0\\,,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{′}\\times f)^{′}(t) & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2-2t\\,,\\,-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩+⟨0\\,,\\,0\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6t+6{t}^{2}\\,,\\,2-2t\\,,\\,-2+6t-4{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186391408 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ ({x}^{3}-2x)^{′} & (10x-5{x}^{3})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3}-2x & 10x-5{x}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2 & 10-15{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-2x)(10-15{x}^{2})-(10x-5{x}^{3})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-2)\\cdot 5(2-3{x}^{2})-5x(2-{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2})-5x({x}^{2}-2)(2-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x-(-15{x}^{5}+40{x}^{3}-20x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"] [17592186391420 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′} & {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡x-({cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"] [17592186391946 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 8 